# Rank 1019 / CVE-2025-57800: Audiobookshelf OIDC token exfiltration. The OIDC
# login route stored a caller-supplied callback in the auth_cb cookie after
# checking only that it is present, and the callback handler then issues
# res.redirect(302, `${req.cookies.auth_cb}?setToken=...&accessToken=...`), so a
# crafted login link sends the victim's tokens to an attacker host and allows
# full account takeover (CWE-601, CWE-598, CWE-523). The fixed revision adds
# isValidWebCallbackUrl(callback, req) and refuses to store a callback that is
# not same-origin.
#
# Binding gap found and closed. Express documents res.redirect([status,] path),
# but only args[0] carried code.RedirectTargetSink, so every two-argument
# redirect is invisible and the vulnerable line goes unreported; the only open
# redirect VyQL reports in this file is the unrelated redirect to the identity
# provider's authorization URL. Binding the second argument as well, for
# res.redirect and the equivalent reply.redirect, makes the vulnerable line fire
# as VYQL-RF-002 from req.cookies.auth_cb into the redirect. Measured before and
# after on two real Express servers: audiobookshelf/server went from 3 findings
# to 4, the one added finding being this CVE, and actual's sync-server stayed at
# 1. The full spec suite passes and the OWASP scores are unchanged at Java +1.00
# and Python +0.90.
#
# ATTENTION nonetheless: the finding does not discriminate this fix. The
# validation the fixed revision adds runs at the cookie write in a different route, while
# the redirect reads the cookie on a later request, so the taint from
# req.cookies.auth_cb into res.redirect is identical either side of the commit.
# Recognising the fixed form would require carrying a control across two requests
# through the cookie store, which taint analysis of a single request path cannot
# do.
#
#  - Measured on the full tree of both revisions (three --cache off runs each):
#    1021 findings at the fix's parent and 1023 at the fix, and every
#    server/Auth.js finding pairs one-to-one across the commit at a shifted
#    line -- the finding above fires at :262 and :272 with the redirect
#    statement byte-identical, and the two extra findings sit on the added
#    guard branches. The flags view matches: the fixed revision only adds
#    InsecureCookie flags on the new early-return sends.
#  - Labelling isValidWebCallbackUrl itself as core.RedirectAllowlist, covers
#    endpoint anchored at its call, still leaves the callback route's finding
#    firing on the fixed tree: the validation runs in the login route's
#    paramsToCookies while the sink is the OAuth callback route, so control and
#    sink share no endpoint and no CFG (explain reports "no guard on sink" on
#    both revisions). The write side carries no vocabulary at all -- res.cookie
#    has no sink binding -- and a write-side sink with no expressible
#    neutralizer would flag every handler that persists a user preference.
#    Discriminating the fix needs a control carried across two routes and two
#    requests through the cookie store.
#
# The tests below pin the newly bound argument position.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-no-discrimination

test "rank1019: redirect with a status argument carries the target in the second position"
  lang javascript
  file server/Auth.js
  expect VYQL-RF-002
  code
  ```
  function handleCallback(req, res, userResponse) {
    if (req.cookies.auth_cb) {
      res.redirect(302, `${req.cookies.auth_cb}?setToken=${userResponse.user.token}`)
    } else {
      res.status(400).send('No callback or already expired')
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1019: a constant redirect target with a status argument is not a finding"
  lang javascript
  file server/Auth.js
  reject VYQL-RF-002
  code
  ```
  function handleCallback(req, res, userResponse) {
    if (req.cookies.auth_cb) {
      res.redirect(302, '/library')
    } else {
      res.status(400).send('No callback or already expired')
    }
  }
  ```
